edit('P0613-Q01',['AC servo or stepper, driving a ball screw with a linear guide','AC servo or stepper, driving a rack and pinion with a linear guide','AC servo only, driving a toothed-belt linear slide','Stepper only, driving a lead screw with a sliding nut'],['The source permits either actuator type and pairs it with a ball screw and linear guide.','Rack-and-pinion conversion is plausible, but it is not the transmission selected on this slide.','A belt slide is plausible but differs from the specified screw, and the source also permits a stepper.','The source does not restrict the actuator to steppers and specifies a ball screw rather than a sliding-nut lead screw.'],stem='Which actuator alternatives and rotary-to-linear transmission are actually specified for joint 3?')
edit('P0613-Q05',['Direct drive is favored for accuracy; a belt can allow remote motor placement and lower moving wrist inertia for high speed','Belt drive is favored for accuracy; a wrist-mounted direct motor is favored for minimum moving wrist inertia','Direct drive is favored for torque multiplication; a belt drive is favored for eliminating transmission compliance','Belt drive is favored for zero speed reduction; direct drive is favored for placing the motor away from the wrist'],['This is the slide’s qualitative comparison: direct drive for accuracy and belt drive for reduced wrist inertia. Remote mounting explains the latter without guaranteeing every design’s performance.','This reverses the source’s emphasized pairing and overlooks the mass carried by a wrist-mounted motor.','A direct drive does not provide reduction-based torque multiplication, and a belt has finite compliance.','Belt ratio need not be unity, and remote motor placement is an advantage of transmission rather than a defining feature of direct drive.'],stem='Which wrist-drive trade-off is emphasized by this lecture, without treating it as a universal performance guarantee?')
